  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1252 AM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nA cluster of disorganized convection continues to backbuild across\nthe far northeastern corner of the state in these early overnight\nhours. The axis of heavier rainfall has generally been just north\nof our forecast area, along the Nebraska border... though radar\nestimates have been as high as 2-3\" in northwest Sedgwick county.\nGuidance pushes most of this activity out of our CWA by about 09z,\nand the Flood Watch will be allowed to expire then. \n\nSunday should be a bit quieter across the plains, as the better\nmoisture pushes westward into the I-25 corridor. With T/Tds near\n90/50 during the afternoon, we'll likely see MLCAPE increase to\naround 1000-1500 J/kg by the afternoon hours. The flow aloft will\nremain rather weak, with 0-6km/effective layer shear sitting near\n20-30kt. The environment would support at least a marginal\nhail/wind threat across the Front Range and urban corridor, but\nwould be conditional on any storms managing to develop during the\nafternoon hours. The HRRR, along with both HREF and REFS members,\nare fairly bullish with this potential, and I would expect at\nleast a couple of thunderstorms to develop around the Denver metro\nbetween roughly 3-8 PM. High temperatures will again reach the\nlow to mid 90s across most of the plains.\n\nMonday still looks to feature the best chance of precipitation\nacross most of the region. With similar moisture available, the\nbiggest change is the addition of some synoptic scale ascent, from\nboth a passing mid-level trough and the right entrance of a\n70-80kt upper jet streak. While it's possible that a majority of\nthe convection ends up to the south and east of Denver - as some\nmodels currently hint at - the overall setup would still favor at\nleast some storms across most of the I-25 corridor. We'll see if\nany obvious trends emerge over the next 24-36 hours.\n\nBy Tuesday and Wednesday, the ridge across the south central CONUS\nshould begin to expand and flatten, bringing weak zonal flow to\nthe state. This should also push the deeper moisture off to our\nsouth/west, and guidance has trended a little drier in this\nperiod. Temperatures look to cool slightly, with highs in the mid\n80s to low 90s. Zonal flow appears likely to persist through the\nrest of the week, which will limit storm chances and keep\ntemperatures fairly steady in the mid 80s to mid 90s across the\nlower elevations. \n\nAs we approach next weekend, guidance continues to hint at a\nstronger trough/closed low pushing across the northern Rockies.\nWhile we'd still end up on the dry side of this system, there's\nbeen a few runs that push some cooler air into the region by\nsometime next weekend. However, while the deterministic forecasts\nhave been optimistic for those looking to get some relief from \nthis pattern, this is far from a guarantee. There is a significant\nspread in 2m temperature forecasts from the past few ensemble\ncycles from the GFS/ECMWF, and a good number of members\n(approximately 40%) would favor above normal temperatures\ncontinuing into mid-September. Still, it's something to watch for\nin an otherwise stagnant pattern.",
